    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Deserts & Drylands: Landscapes, Water and Change",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s dunes, salt flats and stony deserts, and understand how natural aridity differs from drought and desertification."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-the-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A landscape with several languages",
        "content": "A desert in Iran need not resemble a continuous sea of sand. Persian landscape vocabulary distinguishes several surfaces that English descriptions often group together. *Biaban* is a broad term for desert or unsettled country; *kavir* commonly identifies a saline basin floor or playa; and *rig* denotes an accumulation of sand. These distinctions help explain why a journey through desert country can cross firm plains, dunes, and salt-crusted ground in succession. [s1]\n\nThe differences are physical as well as linguistic. A closed basin collects sediment and dissolved salts without an outlet to the sea. Evaporation leaves saline deposits behind. Sand fields, by contrast, reflect sediment transport and accumulation by wind. Treating both as simply empty ground obscures the processes that produced them—and their very different surface conditions. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-contrasts",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Salt country and the Lut",
        "content": "The Great Salt Desert, or Dasht-e Kavir, also preserves evidence of environments far older than its present surface. NASA’s geological interpretation describes ancient evaporite deposits subsequently buried, deformed, and exposed by erosion. Salt can rise through overlying material to form domes; tectonic deformation adds another layer to the landscape’s history. A modern salt flat and an exposed salt dome therefore should not be read as identical features with identical origins. [s3]\n\nSoutheastern Iran’s Dasht-e Lut offers a different combination of forms. UNESCO describes an interior basin where surrounding mountains help create a rain shadow. Strong seasonal winds move sediment and sculpt elongated ridges called yardangs, locally known as kaluts. Elsewhere, stone-covered surfaces and extensive dune fields dominate. These contrasting landforms make the Lut particularly useful for understanding erosion and deposition together: material removed from one place can become part of an accumulation elsewhere. [s4]\n\nThe Lut’s dramatic appearance does not establish an absence of life. UNESCO explicitly recognizes organisms adapted to its harsh conditions while also noting substantial gaps in knowledge of its biodiversity. Geological prominence and ecological understanding are different kinds of evidence. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "drylands-and-people",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Dryland does not mean lifeless",
        "content": "Drylands are defined through the balance between precipitation and potential water loss, rather than through a single visual appearance. FAO’s global assessment includes hyperarid, arid, semi-arid, and dry subhumid zones. Across those categories, drylands contain grasslands, cropland, settlements, woodland, and barren surfaces. Their inclusion in the same climatic grouping does not make them ecologically interchangeable. [s5]\n\nFAO’s assessment used expert interpretation of satellite imagery to distinguish these land uses. That approach matters for readers of Iran’s geography: a pale surface in an image is not, by itself, proof of degradation, and a vegetated surface does not reveal how securely its water supply is maintained. Land cover, climate, and land condition answer related but separate questions. [s5]\n\nHuman settlement also belongs in this landscape. Iran’s caravanserais were roadside stopping places that supplied shelter and provisions to travelers. UNESCO identifies water availability, terrain, and security as factors shaping their locations. Desert travel historically depended on a network of usable places rather than an undifferentiated expanse between cities. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "aridity-drought-degradation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Three changes that should not be confused",
        "content": "Aridity describes a climatic water balance. Drought concerns precipitation significantly below an established normal. Desertification concerns degradation in arid, semi-arid, and dry subhumid areas, arising through climatic variations, human activity, or their interaction. Under the UN Convention to Combat Desertification, degradation includes losses of productivity associated with erosion, deteriorating soil properties, and long-term vegetation loss. [s2][s5]\n\nThese definitions prevent a misleading conclusion: a naturally occurring dune field is not automatically evidence that productive land has recently been destroyed. Equally, land degradation need not produce conspicuous dunes. It can affect cropland or pasture through less photogenic changes in soil and vegetation. Diagnosing it requires evidence of condition and change, rather than a photograph of a dry place. [s2]\n\nThe pages in this branch follow those distinctions. Maranjab and Mesr introduce named desert destinations; Dasht-e Lut explains a major geomorphological landscape; and the desertification and soil-erosion pages address processes. Reading place and process together gives a more useful understanding than treating every dry surface as the same environmental problem.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s5"]
      },
      {
